WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:06.120
Now on to another new addition to UE5, the state tree.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.279
So what is a state tree?

3
00:00:08.279 --> 00:00:09.800
In short, they're a general purpose

4
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.560
hierarchical state machine.

5
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.839
They combine the selectors from behavior trees

6
00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:16.000
with states and transitions that you would usually see

7
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.160
in state machine architectures.

8
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.079
States are arranged in a tree structure

9
00:00:20.079 --> 00:00:22.719
and these states contain evaluators, tasks,

10
00:00:22.719 --> 00:00:26.469
and transitions which we're all gonna discuss shortly.

11
00:00:26.469 --> 00:00:27.829
A selection of states can be triggered

12
00:00:27.829 --> 00:00:29.550
at any location on the tree

13
00:00:29.589 --> 00:00:32.920
but it initially starts from the root of the tree.

14
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.159
In order to create a state tree asset however,

15
00:00:35.159 --> 00:00:37.880
you must first select a schema for your state tree

16
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.640
and current schemas are available.

17
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.719
The first one being the mass state tree

18
00:00:41.719 --> 00:00:46.250
and the last one being the state tree component.

19
00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.719
Let's take a look at the anatomy of a state tree.

20
00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:51.859
First of all, in figure one, we have the root

21
00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:53.799
and this is the first state selected

22
00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:56.469
when the state tree is running.

23
00:00:56.469 --> 00:00:58.829
Next in figure two, we have the selector state

24
00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:02.070
and this refers to a state that has child states.

25
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.450
This state will never be selected directly

26
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:08.239
and selection will continue to one of its child states.

27
00:01:08.239 --> 00:01:10.959
In figure three, we can see the state enter condition

28
00:01:10.959 --> 00:01:12.739
and this refers to the list of conditions

29
00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:16.010
that determine when a state can be selected.

30
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.290
In figure four, we have a task

31
00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.010
and this refers to a set of actions

32
00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.569
that belonged to a state

33
00:01:21.569 --> 00:01:25.019
and are executed when the state becomes active.

34
00:01:25.019 --> 00:01:26.700
Lastly, we have transition

35
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.060
and this defines the condition

36
00:01:28.099 --> 00:01:30.900
that triggers the state selection process.

37
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.540
A transition is triggered when a task completes,

38
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.719
succeeds or fails or when a monitor condition succeeds.

39
00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:40.560
A state contains a list of conditions

40
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:41.879
which must be met in order

41
00:01:41.879 --> 00:01:45.640
to consider the state for activation.

42
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.019
During the selection process,

43
00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:49.299
each state's conditions are evaluated.

44
00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:51.739
If they pass, then the selection process proceeds

45
00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:54.599
to the state's child states if there are any.

46
00:01:54.599 --> 00:01:56.060
If there are no child states,

47
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.120
then the state itself will be activated.

48
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.180
Each state consists of evaluators, tasks and transitions.

49
00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.840
The data in the state tree flows from root to its leaves.

50
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.000
This means the data exposed by any state

51
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.490
will be available for the other states down the tree.

52
00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.930
It's important to note that when a state is selected,

53
00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:16.729
it will activate all of the states

54
00:02:16.729 --> 00:02:19.900
from the root to the leaf state.

55
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.800
All tasks are executed for all active states,

56
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.240
starting from the root down to the selected state.

57
00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.259
A state can contain multiple tasks

58
00:02:28.259 --> 00:02:30.020
that are all run concurrently

59
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.419
for as long as the state remains active.

60
00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:35.860
A task in a state tree is similar in concept

61
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.099
to tasks in a behavior tree.

62
00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:40.180
A task is an action that is executed

63
00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.560
when its owning state is activated.

64
00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.379
It provides an output to the state tree.

65
00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:47.219
Some common examples would be the selection of a target

66
00:02:47.219 --> 00:02:49.659
or the playing of an animation.

67
00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:52.719
All tasks inside a state will run concurrently.

68
00:02:52.719 --> 00:02:55.000
Once the first completes, it will trigger a transition

69
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.560
which can result in the selection of a new state entirely.

70
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.240
Global tasks provide a way to run regular tasks

71
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.159
that are associated with the tree

72
00:03:03.159 --> 00:03:05.870
rather than a specific state.

73
00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:07.849
These tasks can contain variables

74
00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:10.349
and execute custom code when the tree starts,

75
00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:12.710
ends, and ticks.

76
00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.750
Gathered data can be used during state selection

77
00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.110
and will be available, as the name suggests,

78
00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:19.759
globally throughout the state tree.

79
00:03:19.759 --> 00:03:21.699
For example, we could have a global task

80
00:03:21.699 --> 00:03:24.520
that determines the current time of day.

81
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.060
Since the global tasks are started

82
00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.120
before the root state in the tree,

83
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.360
this information would be available when the tree starts

84
00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.569
and during the first state selection.

85
00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:35.990
If this state tree was controlling an AI,

86
00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.210
the state selection could result in different behavior

87
00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.960
during the night versus the day.

88
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.939
A transition is pretty self-explanatory.

89
00:03:43.939 --> 00:03:46.120
It can point to another state in the tree

90
00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.599
which will trigger a state selection process once again.

91
00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:51.620
A transition can contain trigger conditions

92
00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.030
that need to be met before the transition can occur.

93
00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.669
Transitions are evaluated starting with the leaf state

94
00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:00.289
and progressing upwards towards the root.

95
00:04:00.289 --> 00:04:03.110
During this process, the first transition that succeeds

96
00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:06.169
and leads to a state selection will be selected.

97
00:04:06.169 --> 00:04:10.580
This hierarchy allows for grouping of common transitions.

98
00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.659
Completion transitions, however, now trigger at the state

99
00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:16.579
where the task that is completed is located.

100
00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:18.740
For example, if the execution is currently

101
00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:22.699
at root guard leaf and the task at guard state completes,

102
00:04:22.699 --> 00:04:27.629
transitions on guard and root are handled skipping leaf.

103
00:04:27.649 --> 00:04:29.310
It's important to note that tick

104
00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.069
and event triggered transitions will have a precedence

105
00:04:32.069 --> 00:04:33.670
over completion transitions.
